TY  - JOUR
T1  - Transforming natural language into code: advancing automated code synthesis for software development
AN  - 3259413585
AB  - Automated code generation from natural language is a growing challenge in software engineering, enabling the direct translation of requirements into executable code. Machine learning-based automatic code synthesis emerged as a key solution, with transformer-based models playing a pivotal role in enhancing this process. This paper addresses the challenges of generating accurate, functional code efficiently. This paper presents a transformer-based T5 model fine-tuned on the multilingual XLCoST dataset for code generation. The model is evaluated using a novel systematic multi-level evaluation framework. The framework evaluates the model&rsquo;s across three distinct task complexity levels ranging from beginner to advanced. The evaluation results highlight the model&rsquo;s versatility and robustness. Furthermore, the model achieves a BLEU score of 30.093, demonstrating competitive performance compared to open-source baselines. The model&rsquo;s code generation abilities accelerates software development by reducing manual coding efforts, thereby improving workflow efficiency and software quality. These results confirm that transformer-based models are effective tools for automating software development, with promising implications for the future.
